WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.682
To compare the tests fairly,

00:00:01.762 --> 00:00:05.772
the authors first run a controlled competition on
synthetic data.

00:00:05.852 --> 00:00:08.792
They pit a heterogeneous stochastic block model,

00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:12.504
which has an uneven degree distribution but no
communities,

00:00:12.584 --> 00:00:16.720
against an assortative one, which has communities
but even degrees,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.188
holding edge density fixed so the two are genuinely
hard to tell apart.

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:28.612
They vary graph size from one hundred twenty-eight
to two hundred fifty-six nodes and the number of graphs per sample.

00:00:28.692 --> 00:00:33.646
They then move to real data: genetic-interaction
networks of Arabidopsis, mouse,

00:00:33.726 --> 00:00:36.352
human, and rat from the FunCoup repository,

00:00:36.432 --> 00:00:41.700
again matched in edge density so that separating
them demands higher-order structure.
